# Public semantic review of the new OAuth exact-set predicate

The exact `code/token/none` equality is **not independently qualified as mandatory original repair behavior**. It remains a new author-imposed assumption. Preserve its observed result as a diagnostic and keep the original oracle unchanged.

The pristine public source provides stronger evidence than a dictionary key alone: `Server` routes `none` to the authorization-code handler; validation accepts that response type; the shared response builder suppresses code/token output for `none`, retaining state. However, the grant class declares `response_types = ['code']`, server documentation requires an application validator, and the example instantiates the abstract `RequestValidator` rather than proving a deployed authorization flow. No exposed library contract was found requiring default discovery metadata to equal every internal dispatch key.

RFC 8414 defines discovery metadata in terms of response types supported by the authorization server. It does not specify how a library must derive that list from its dispatch table. [RFC 8414 §2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8414.html#section-2).

The official response-type specification defines and registers `none`; it generally returns no authorization code or tokens and preserves supplied state. Therefore, a claim that `none` is universally forbidden in this metadata would also be unsupported. [OAuth Multiple Response Type Encoding Practices §4](https://openid.net/specs/oauth-v2-multiple-response-types-1_0.html#none).

This review does not establish that omission is correct for a deployment that actually supports `none`. It separates a plausible new default-advertising requirement from a demonstrated historical repair obligation. It uses only pristine public source/docs and official standards, with no protected predicate or reference-code basis and no new target execution. Public files, document bodies and SHA256 receipts are retained here.
